istribution of electricity; solar power facilities; and distribution of natural gas. The company also operates regulated water utilities that provides water services to residential, commercial, industrial, municipal and fire protection, and other customers in Connecticut, Massachusetts, and New Hampshire. The company was formerly known as Northeast Utilities and changed its name to Eversource Energy in April 2015. Eversource Energy was incorporated in 1927 and is headquartered in Springfield, Massachusetts.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Joseph R. Nolan Jr. | President, CEO & Chairman | Age 61 | Compensation: $4.5M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 10K people. Revenue per employee of $1.3M ranks in the above the sector median among Utilities peers, with revenue growing 8%/yr across its filed history. Stock-based compensation of $3K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 4\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags elevated compensation governance risk (7/10).","financial_summary":["Eversource Energy (D/B/A) is a large-cap utilities business with a $27.0B market cap on $13.5B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +3% over the past three years and +5% annually across the full decade, which puts it in mature, slow-growth territory.","Returns on capital run at 5% against a 6% cost of capital, which means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests.","The company is burning through $45M of cash, and without positive owner earnings any valuation rests on hope rather than evidence.","On the balance sheet, debt is heavy at 5.2\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and leverage like that is what turns a bad year into a permanent loss, and thin 2.4\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Shareholder yield of 2.3% reflects a genuine commitment to returning capital rather than empire-building, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Altman Z in the distress zone (0.8) \u2014 the kind of balance-sheet risk that can make a loss permanent","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Priced near or above estimated fair value, so the margin of safety has been bid away","sev":"amber","cat":"valuation","magnitude":0.06851685659506827},{"text":"Insiders are net sellers (92% sell ratio, net $3.3M) \u2014 the people closest to the business are reducing their personal stake","sev":"amber","cat":"insider"},{"text":"Regulatory rate-case risk and rising cost of capital \u2014 the allowed return is set by commissions, and the business lives or dies by that negotiation.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Grid modernisation capex burden \u2014 aging infrastructure requires massive reinvestment that regulators may not fully allow into rate base, compressing earned returns.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Wildfire and climate liability \u2014 extreme weather events create catastrophic, uninsurable losses that can push even investment-grade utilities toward restructuring.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Returns on capital have been remarkably steady for 5 years \u2014 durability is the real test of a moat","27 consecutive years of dividend payments \u2014 an operating record that makes the dividend claim credible"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"How Softer Earnings At Eversource Energy (ES) Has Changed Its Investment Story","source":"simplywall.st","link":"https://simplywall.st/stocks/us/utilities/nyse-es/eversource-energy/news/how-softer-earnings-at-eversource-energy-es-has-changed-its","date":"2026-11-13","timestamp":1794583725.527,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.34,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":["earnings"]},{"title":"Is Eversource (NYSE:ES) Leaning Into the New England Grid?","source":"kalki